From an application → Oracle Database perspective, the flow looks like this:

Without Oracle Native Network Encryption (NNE):

Application sends a SQL query to the Oracle Database.
The query travels over the network in plain text.
Oracle Database processes the query.
Database sends the results back.
The results also travel in plain text.
Anyone who intercepts the network traffic could potentially read the data.

With Oracle Native Network Encryption (NNE):

Application sends a SQL query.
Oracle Client (JDBC/OCI/SQL*Plus, etc.) encrypts the query.
The encrypted data travels over the network.
Oracle Database decrypts the query.
Database executes the query.
Oracle Database encrypts the query results.
The encrypted results travel back over the network.
Oracle Client decrypts the results.
The application receives the original data.


Application
     │
     │ Plain data
     ▼
Oracle Client
     │
     │ 🔒 Encrypted (NNE)
=========================== Network ===========================
     │
     ▼
Oracle Database
     │
     │ Execute SQL
     ▼
Oracle Database
     │
     │ 🔒 Encrypted results
=========================== Network ===========================
     │
     ▼
Oracle Client
     │
     │ Plain data
     ▼
